<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<!-- Chapter 10 exercises.

     One flat list, numbered in order. Which exercises get assigned
     together lives in the course schedule, not here.

     Adapted from the previous instructor's Lesson 13 written homework:
     Exercise 1 = old Exercise 1, plus new part (d) on Theorems 10.1 and
     10.2. Exercise 2 = old Problem 1 (same game), plus new part (d) and a
     closing remark on the intuition. Exercise 3 = old Exercise 2 (the
     Stag Hunt with Easily Spooked Hares, a = c, same game as Ch. 8
     Exercise hw-ess-theorem-8-1), plus new parts (e), (f), and (g). Exercise 4 replaces old
     Problem 2 (Jacobian and eigenvalues): same F/S/P game, but only
     uses Theorems 10.1-10.4, no multivariable analysis.

     The old homework called the game in Exercises 1 and 2 a
     "coordination game," but it is case (4) of Figure 10.3, so that
     label was dropped.

     Confidence checks: only selected parts have a short inline
     <solution> (1(b), 2(a), 3(c), 3(d)). Full answers are on
     ch10-answers.ptx.

     Uses Sections 10.1-10.4 and 10.6 (Theorems 10.1-10.4), Theorem 8.5,
     and the phase line from Chapter 9.

     Add more by appending another <exercise> below; numbering of the
     existing ones will not shift. -->

<exercises xml:id="ch10-exercises">
  <title>Exercises</title>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-replicator-derive">
    <title>Deriving the Replicator Equation</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Consider the following symmetric game:
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-replicator-ab">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>b</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>a</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>b</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, 4)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-derive-system">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>\sigma = p_1 a + p_2 b</m> denote the population state.
          Derive the replicator system for this game (a system of two
          differential equations <m>\dot{p}_1 = g(p_1, p_2)</m> and
          <m>\dot{p}_2 = h(p_1, p_2)</m>).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-derive-reduce">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>p_1 = p</m>, so that <m>p_2 = 1 - p</m>. Reduce the system
          from part (a) to a single differential equation
          <m>\dot{p} = f(p)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          You should expect <m>f(p)</m> to have both <m>p</m> and
          <m>1 - p</m> as factors.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\dot{p} = p(1 - p)(4 - 5p)</m>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-derive-equilibria">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the equilibria of <m>\dot{p} = f(p)</m> in <m>[0, 1]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-derive-theorems">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without using your formula for <m>f(p)</m>, explain why we already
          know that <m>p = 0</m> and <m>p = 1</m> must be equilibria. Which
          theorem tells us this? Then explain which theorem tells us that the
          interior equilibrium (the one between 0 and 1) is where both
          strategies earn the same payoff against the population state at that
          equilibrium, and why.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-replicator-phase-line">
    <title>Phase Line and Stability</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is a continuation of <xref ref="hw-replicator-derive"/>, with
        the same game.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-phase-line-sketch">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using your results from <xref ref="hw-replicator-derive"/>, sketch
          a graph of <m>f(p)</m> and use it to sketch the phase line on
          <m>[0, 1]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longrightarrow\!\!\longrightarrow \underset{4/5}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>,
          so <m>p = \frac{4}{5}</m> is stable and <m>p = 0</m> and
          <m>p = 1</m> are unstable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-phase-line-behavior">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Summarize in words the behavior of a typical solution that does not
          begin at an equilibrium. What happens to a solution that begins
          with <m>p</m> near 0? What about one that begins with <m>p</m> near
          1?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-phase-line-ess">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Analyze the game using Theorem 8.5: which case is this game in, and
          what does that case tell you? Then explain the connection between
          the results of that analysis and part (b).
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-phase-line-start">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose the population starts exactly at the interior equilibrium.
          What happens? What if it starts just below it?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <em>Remark:</em> In Microsoft vs. Apple, the mixed equilibrium was
        unstable, but here it is stable. You can see why from the game itself.
        When almost everyone plays <m>a</m>, a typical opponent plays
        <m>a</m>, so <m>b</m> earns 1 while <m>a</m> earns 0, and <m>b</m>
        spreads. When almost everyone plays <m>b</m>, <m>a</m> earns 4 while
        <m>b</m> earns 0, and <m>a</m> spreads. Either way, the population is
        pushed toward a mix of the two strategies.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases">
    <title>A Game Outside the Four Cases</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        This is the Stag Hunt with Easily Spooked Hares from Section 8.6.3 of
        the book, which you analyzed in <xref ref="hw-ess-theorem-8-1"/>
        (there, with <m>a</m> = stag and <m>b</m> = hare). Two hunters
        together can kill a stag, worth 4 to each of them. A hunter alone
        can't kill a stag, but can catch four hares, each worth 1. If both
        hunters go after the hares, the hares run away and each hunter catches
        only one. The strategies are hunt the stag (<m>s</m>) or hunt the
        hares (<m>h</m>):
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-replicator-sh">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>s</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>h</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>s</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 4)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 4)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>h</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(4, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(1, 1)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-reduce">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Derive the replicator system for this game and reduce it to a
          single differential equation <m>\dot{p} = f(p)</m>, where
          <m>p</m> is the share using <m>s</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-equilibria">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the equilibria of <m>\dot{p} = f(p)</m> in <m>[0, 1]</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-phase-line">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Sketch a graph of <m>f(p)</m> and use it to draw the phase line.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <m>\underset{0}{\bullet} \longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow\!\!\longleftarrow \underset{1}{\bullet}</m>,
          so <m>p = 0</m> is stable and <m>p = 1</m> is unstable.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-interpret">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Give a brief interpretation of your results from part (c).
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Unless everyone starts with <m>s</m>, <m>s</m> dies out and the
          population approaches all-<m>h</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-symmetric-ne">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="hw-ess-theorem-8-1"/>, you found that <m>(s, s)</m>
          and <m>(h, h)</m> are Nash equilibria. Which theorem from this
          chapter, combined with your answer to part (b), tells us that there
          are no other symmetric Nash equilibria? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-ess">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In <xref ref="hw-ess-theorem-8-1"/>, you found the evolutionarily
          stable states of this game. Compare them with the stability of the
          equilibria from part (c). Then explain why this game does not fit
          any of the four cases in <xref ref="table-ch10-stable-vs-ess"/>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-outside-cases-thm-10-3">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the all-<m>s</m> state, where <m>\sigma = s</m>.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Compute <m>\pi_1(h, \sigma)</m>, the payoff an <m>h</m>-player
                would earn, and <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m>, the population's
                average payoff.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Theorem 10.3 needs
                <m>\pi_1(h, \sigma) \gt \pi_1(\sigma, \sigma)</m>. Does that
                hold here? Does Theorem 10.3 apply?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                From our analysis earlier, the all-<m>s</m> state is unstable.
                Why doesn't this contradict Theorem 10.3?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ============================================================ -->
  <exercise xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp">
    <title>Using the Theorems with Three Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        In an earlier homework, we considered a Prisoner's Dilemma repeated
        for two stages, with three strategies:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              Always cooperate (<m>F</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Always defect (<m>S</m>)
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              Tit-for-tat (<m>P</m>): choose <m>C</m> in stage 1. In stage 2,
              copy the other player's move from stage 1.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
        This led to the following game:
      </p>
      <table xml:id="table-hw-replicator-fsp">
        <title>The game</title>
        <tabular row-headers="yes" halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
          <col halign="left" right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col right="minor"/>
          <col/>
          <row header="yes" bottom="minor">
            <cell></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>F</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>S</m></cell>
            <cell>Player 2: <m>P</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>F</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(6, 6)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-2, 10)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(6, 6)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row bottom="minor">
            <cell>Player 1: <m>S</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(10, -2)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(0, 0)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(5, -1)</m></cell>
          </row>
          <row>
            <cell>Player 1: <m>P</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(6, 6)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(-1, 5)</m></cell>
            <cell><m>(6, 6)</m></cell>
          </row>
        </tabular>
      </table>
      <p>
        With three strategies, the replicator system has two variables, so
        we won't analyze it directly. Instead, answer each part using
        Theorems 10.1 through 10.4.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp-equilibria">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use Theorem 10.1 to name three equilibria of the replicator system
          without any computation.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The pure states: all-<m>F</m>, all-<m>S</m>, and all-<m>P</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp-all-f">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the all-<m>F</m> state, where everyone always cooperates.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                Use best response (box each player's best responses in the
                payoff matrix) to show that <m>(F, F)</m> is not a Nash
                equilibrium.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                By Theorem 10.1, the all-<m>F</m> state is an equilibrium of
                the replicator system. Why doesn't this contradict Theorem
                10.2?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp-invade">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Again consider the all-<m>F</m> state, where <m>\sigma = F</m>.
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                A quick computation shows that <m>\pi_1(S, \sigma) = 10</m> and
                <m>\pi_1(\sigma, \sigma) = 6</m>. What does Theorem 10.3 tell
                you about the behavior over time of solutions that contain some
                <m>S</m>-players?
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                What happens if the population starts at all-<m>F</m> with no
                <m>S</m>-players at all? Which part of Theorem 10.1 tells you
                this?
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp-all-s">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Notice that <m>(S, S)</m> is a strict Nash equilibrium. Use one
          theorem from Chapter 8 and another from Chapter 10 to conclude that
          the all-<m>S</m> state is an asymptotically stable equilibrium of
          the replicator system.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="hw-replicator-theorems-fsp-all-p">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Notice that the all-<m>P</m> state is not evolutionarily stable. A
          quick check shows that <m>(P, P)</m> is a Nash equilibrium.
          However, it fails condition (2) of Theorem 8.1: taking
          <m>\tau = F</m>, we have <m>\pi_1(F, P) = \pi_1(P, P) = 6</m>, but
          <m>\pi_1(P, F) = \pi_1(F, F) = 6</m>, so <m>\pi_1(P, F)</m> is not
          greater than <m>\pi_1(F, F)</m>.
        </p>
        <p>
          A classmate says, <q>Since the all-<m>P</m> state isn't
          evolutionarily stable, Theorem 10.4 shows that it is not asymptotically
          stable.</q>
          Explain the error.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>

    <conclusion>
      <p>
        <em>Remark:</em> The classmate's conclusion happens to be true, even
        though the reasoning is not. Section 10.7 of the book analyzes this
        game with slightly different payoffs, and shows that populations near
        the all-<m>P</m> state stay near it, but don't return exactly to
        all-<m>P</m>.
      </p>
    </conclusion>
  </exercise>
</exercises>
